{"problem": "poly_from_roots\n\nCompute a polynomial's coefficients from its roots.\n\n>>> roots = [5, -4, 3]            # (x - 5) * (x + 4) * (x - 3)\n>>> poly_from_roots(roots)  # x\u00b3 - 4 x\u00b2 - 17 x + 60\n[1, -4, -17, 60]\n\nNote that polynomial coefficients are specified in descending power order.\n\nSupports all numeric types: int, float, complex, Decimal, Fraction.", "resolution": "def poly_from_roots(roots):\n    \"\"\"Compute a polynomial's coefficients from its roots.\n\n    >>> roots = [5, -4, 3]            # (x - 5) * (x + 4) * (x - 3)\n    >>> poly_from_roots(roots)  # x\u00b3 - 4 x\u00b2 - 17 x + 60\n    [1, -4, -17, 60]\n\n    Note that polynomial coefficients are specified in descending power order.\n\n    Supports all numeric types: int, float, complex, Decimal, Fraction.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # This recipe differs from the one in itertools docs in that it\n    # applies list() after each call to linear_convolve().  This avoids\n    # hitting stack limits with nested generators.\n\n    poly = [1]\n    for root in roots:\n        poly = list(linear_convolve(poly, (1, -root)))\n    return poly\n", "text": "poly_from_roots"}